<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  tingra-app.entitlements
  tingra-app

  Created by Larry Aasen on 2026-08-01.
  Copyright © 2026 Larry Aasen.
  SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT

  Notarization requires the hardened runtime, which denies camera and
  microphone access unless the binary opts in — hence the two device
  entitlements. disable-library-validation lets the host load a camera's
  CMIOExtension DAL plug-in, and third-party plug-ins generally, signed by
  a different team than this app (without it, CMIOGraphStart fails and no
  camera ever streams; the same fix appears in
  apps/tingra-cli/tingra-cli.entitlements and
  apps/tingra-cameras/tingra-cameras/tingra-cameras.entitlements). No
  sandbox entitlement: Tingra is deliberately unsandboxed. Screen Recording
  needs no entitlement — it is TCC-only.

  keychain-access-groups is back as of 2026-08-30, and it is not optional
  polish: without a keychain group this app cannot use the data-protection
  keychain at all, so every stream key it filed was rejected with
  errSecMissingEntitlement (-34018) and kept only in memory for the session
  (DESTINATIONS.md, "What the probe measured"). Declaring the group is what
  makes the app's own secure storage work; sharing it with a second Tingra
  binary is a later benefit, not the reason it is here.

  Note $(AppIdentifierPrefix), not $(TeamIdentifierPrefix) — they are the same
  string on most accounts and are not the same here, because this one carries
  a legacy App ID prefix distinct from its team identifier. The entitlement
  must name the prefix the provisioning profile actually grants, which is the
  App ID prefix; $(TeamIdentifierPrefix) fails the build with "Provisioning
  profile ... doesn't match the entitlements file's value". Either way no
  literal prefix appears in this file: the variable expands at signing
  (CLAUDE.md, "Signing"), and KeychainSecureStorage.sharedAccessGroup() reads
  the expanded value back out of the running binary, matching on the suffix so
  it never has to know which prefix it got.

  The build needs no manual provisioning step: with this entitlement present,
  automatic signing resolves the team's wildcard profile, which grants the
  whole <prefix>.* keychain group space, and embeds it in the bundle. CI is
  unaffected — it builds CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=NO, and entitlements bind at
  signing. Verify a build with codesign -d --entitlements - on the bundle,
  never by whether it launched: Xcode silently drops an entitlement it cannot
  provision, which is how v0.1.1's cause stayed hidden.
-->
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.device.camera</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
    <true/>
    <key>keychain-access-groups</key>
    <array>
        <string>$(AppIdentifierPrefix)com.moonwink.tingra.shared</string>
    </array>
</dict>
</plist>
